4. Users

The application has a user system.

A user has a profile containing information such as:

username
profile information
avatar
followers/following
coins
winning streak
clothing/wardrobe information

Users can:

find other users
follow/unfollow users
view other users' profiles
view their outfits
potentially view their wardrobe if the user allows it
challenge friends/followed users
vote in competitions

For the Telegram MVP, Telegram itself can provide the user's identity, so we don't necessarily need to build a traditional email/password registration experience.

5. Avatar and clothing system

A major part of the application is the user's digital avatar.

The user can customize their avatar using digital clothing.

We don't have the time or design resources to create all clothing ourselves, so the plan is to use ready-made 2D avatar/clothing assets.

The clothing system contains concepts such as:

Avatar
   │
   ├── Hair
   ├── Top
   ├── Bottom
   ├── Shoes
   └── Accessories

Users can obtain clothing from the shop.

Shop flow
Shop
 ↓
Filter/search clothing
 ↓
Select item
 ↓
Preview item on avatar
 ↓
Item appears with locked/preview state
 ↓
Purchase with coins
 ↓
Choose:
   ├── Equip immediately
   └── Put in wardrobe

The wardrobe stores clothing owned by the user.

Users can later equip/unequip clothing.

6. Coin economy

Coins are the application's internal reward currency.

The main intended way of receiving coins is through Coca-Cola product purchases.

For example:

Purchase Coca-Cola
       ↓
Scan/redeem code
       ↓
Validation
       ↓
+50 coins

Coins can then be spent on:

clothing
voting in competitions

The competition voting system uses a prize pool.

For example:

User A contributes 10 coins
User B contributes 10 coins
Other users vote
        ↓
Prize pool = 20+ coins
        ↓
Winner receives prize

For the MVP, the intended voting rule is:

One vote costs one coin.

The winner receives the accumulated prize pool.

The purpose is to make voting more interesting while creating another reason for users to participate in the ecosystem.

7. QR / barcode purchase system

Originally, the plan was to have unique QR codes associated with Coca-Cola products.

Each unique code would be stored in the database and could only be redeemed once.

Conceptually:

Unique QR #001
Unique QR #002
Unique QR #003
...

When a user scans:

QR
 ↓
Backend
 ↓
Does this code exist?
 ↓
Has it already been redeemed?
 ↓
Is it valid/expired?
 ↓
Redeem
 ↓
Create coin transaction
Current Cola Fest problem

We may not be able to convince Coca-Cola to manufacture special QR codes for the event.

We therefore considered using existing Coca-Cola barcodes.

However, normal product barcodes are generally product identifiers, not unique identifiers for individual bottles.

For example:

Bottle A ─┐
Bottle B ─┼── same product barcode
Bottle C ─┤
Bottle D ─┘

Therefore, simply storing a barcode and preventing it from being scanned twice would not work correctly.

The current investigation is around finding a way to verify an actual purchase, potentially through a unique purchase/receipt identifier or an event-specific mechanism.

This issue is not completely finalized yet.

