10. Competition system

Competitions are one of the most important features.

A user can challenge another user to an outfit battle.

The general process:

User A
 ↓
Select User B
 ↓
Choose competition duration
 ↓
Send request
 ↓
User B receives request
 ↓
Accept / Decline

Currently planned duration presets include:

30 minutes
1 hour
6 hours
24 hours

The challenger chooses the duration.

11. Competition requests

A competition request is separate from an actual competition.

The request has states such as:

pending
accepted
declined
cancelled

Only after acceptance does an actual competition get created.

An important business rule is:

A user can have only one active competition at a time.

Another rule:

If one competition request is accepted, other pending requests involving either participant are cancelled.

This prevents a user from simultaneously entering multiple active battles.

12. Active competition

Once a request is accepted:

Competition
├── challenger
├── opponent
├── start time
├── duration
├── end time
├── status
├── prize pool
├── total votes
└── winner

The competition runs for the selected duration.

Users' followers can vote for either participant.

Example:

🔥 OUTFIT BATTLE


Uzeir        VS        Ali


[Vote Uzeir]
[Vote Ali]

Each vote costs one coin.

The coin is added to the competition's prize pool.

13. Competition result

When the competition timer ends:

Votes are counted
        ↓
Participant with more votes wins
        ↓
Winner receives prize pool
        ↓
Winner's winning streak increases
        ↓
Loser's winning streak resets

Example:

Uzeir: 72 votes
Ali:   51 votes


🏆 Uzeir wins


Prize pool: 123 coins


Uzeir winning streak: 4

The winning streak is intended to provide another motivation for users to continue competing.

14. Competition sections

The application has two major competition views.

My Competitions

Shows competitions directly involving the current user.

If the user has no active competition, the interface can show an empty state.

There can also be:

competition requests
pending challenges
active competition
Other Competitions

This shows competitions involving people the user follows.

For the MVP, we are intentionally not implementing a recommendation algorithm.

The initial approach is simply to show relevant competitions from the user's social network.

15. Voting economy

The voting system is intentionally simple for the MVP.

1 vote = 1 coin

The coin goes into the competition prize pool.

At the end:

Winner → entire prize pool

The goal isn't to create a sophisticated financial system.

The goal is to test whether spending earned coins on competitions makes the experience more engaging.

The project intentionally doesn't overcomplicate coin transfers between users at the MVP stage.

16. Notifications

Notifications are important because competitions are time-based.

For the Telegram MVP, the Telegram bot handles notifications.

Examples:

Challenge

⚔️ You have been challenged by Uzeir!

Competition accepted

✅ Ali accepted your competition!

Competition ending

⏰ Your competition ends in 10 minutes!

Result

🏆 You won the competition!

The bot can provide a button that opens the Mini App.

For the future Flutter application, Telegram will eventually be replaced by a mobile push notification system.

